        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_267"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_268"/>
        <dc:subject>
           <z:AutomaticTag><rdf:value>DESeq2 Package</rdf:value></z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Differential Expression Analysis</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Negative Binomial Generalize Linear Model</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Observe Fisher Information</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>
           <z:AutomaticTag><rdf:value>Read Count</rdf:value></z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:title>Moderated estimation of fold change and dispersion for RNA-seq data with DESeq2</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>In comparative high-throughput sequencing assays, a fundamental task is the analysis of count data, such as read counts per gene in RNA-seq, for evidence of systematic changes across experimental conditions. Small replicate numbers, discreteness, large dynamic range and the presence of outliers require a suitable statistical approach. We present DESeq2, a method for differential analysis of count data, using shrinkage estimation for dispersions and fold changes to improve stability and interpretability of estimates. This enables a more quantitative analysis focused on the strength rather than the mere presence of differential expression. The DESeq2 package is available at http://www.bioconductor.org/packages/release/bioc/html/DESeq2.html.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2014-12-05</dc:date>
        <z:libraryCatalog>BioMed Central</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>https://doi.org/10.1186/s13059-014-0550-8</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2024-10-29 08:54:57</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>Number: 12</dc:description>
        <bib:pages>550</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
